PRD: AstroTwin — Astronaut Health Digital Twin
Nov 14, 2026 · @Aditya
Overview
AstroTwin is a digital twin of each crew member's body. It predicts how bone, muscle and the heart weaken in microgravity, and how fast an injury will heal when there is no hospital nearby. A flight surgeon or the crew can test treatment and exercise plans on the twin before trying them on the person.
Problem. In orbit, the body is already breaking down: astronauts lose bone and muscle every week without heavy exercise. An injury such as a sprained ankle or a fractured wrist adds to that loss and heals in an unusual environment. Today, ground teams judge recovery mostly from experience, crew check-ins and standard protocols. There is no tool that combines a person's baseline, the mission timeline and the injury into one forecast.
Why now. Commercial stations, private missions and Artemis lunar flights will put many more people in space. These crews include older and less-trained flyers, and lunar or Mars crews face communication delays that make real-time help from the ground harder. Health monitoring has to become personal, predictive and partly self-serve for the crew.
Goals and non-goals
Goals
Forecast each crew member's bone density, muscle mass and cardiovascular fitness, day by day, across a whole mission.
Forecast recovery time for common in-flight injuries, adjusted for microgravity and the person's current condition.
Let users compare countermeasure plans (exercise, nutrition, medication) on the twin and see the predicted difference.
Flag rising risk early, before it shows up as a symptom or a failed fitness test.
Scale from one crew of 4 to dozens of crews on commercial stations, the Moon and Mars.
Non-goals
Diagnosing injuries. AstroTwin forecasts recovery after a clinician or crew medical officer has identified the injury.
Replacing the flight surgeon. Every recommendation is advice that a person approves.
Being a certified medical device in the hackathon version. The model is a research prototype.
Covering every condition. Version 1 covers bone, muscle and cardiovascular deconditioning plus a small set of musculoskeletal injuries.
Target users
The primary user is the flight surgeon on the ground. The crew medical officer in orbit is a close second, and becomes the main user on missions too far away for real-time contact.
