User stories
The core scenario is an injury mid-mission: an astronaut sprains an ankle on day 40 of a 180-day mission, and the team must decide how to keep them fit enough to return to Earth safely.
As a flight surgeon, I enter the injury and its severity, and see the predicted recovery curve compared with a healthy baseline.
As a flight surgeon, I test two plans, such as modified resistance exercise versus rest, and see which one keeps bone and muscle loss lower by landing day.
As a crew medical officer, I get a daily plan and a short explanation, so I can act during a communication blackout.
As a crew member, I see my own body status as a simple 3D view and know whether I am on track.
As a station health lead, I see which customers are trending toward a risk threshold this week.
As a mission planner, I check whether a crew member will be fit for a scheduled spacewalk or landing.
As a researcher, I export forecasts and outcomes to compare with real data and improve the model.
