Science model and data
The twin starts from published rates of loss in spaceflight, then adjusts them for the person, the countermeasures and any injury. A simple, explainable model is more credible to judges than a black-box one, and it can be built in a weekend.
Core model, per tissue. Each tissue (bone, muscle, aerobic fitness) loses a share of its value every day in microgravity. Countermeasures reduce that rate, an injury raises it locally, and the value recovers after landing.
V(t+1) = V(t) \cdot \left(1 - r \cdot (1 - c) \cdot m_{\text{injury}} \cdot m_{\text{person}}\right)
Here V is the tissue value, r is the base daily loss rate, c is countermeasure effectiveness (0 to 1), m injury is the extra loss from an injured limb not being used, and m person adjusts for age and baseline fitness.
Starting values (approximate, from memory; verify against the datasets below before the pitch):
Bone: weight-bearing bones such as the hip and spine lose roughly 1 to 1.5% of their density per month without effective countermeasures.
Muscle: leg and back muscles can lose a large share of their mass and strength on long missions without exercise; modern resistance exercise reduces this a lot.
Aerobic fitness: fitness drops in the first weeks of flight, and the heart has to readapt to gravity after landing.
Injury healing: bone and soft-tissue healing is thought to be slower in microgravity, based mainly on animal studies. Model this as a configurable slow-down factor.
