Individual astronaut medical records are private, so the demo uses realistic made-up astronauts built from published averages.
System architecture
One Python service runs the twin, and a React app sends it inputs and draws the results. Model values come from NASA datasets and are stored as settings, so they can be updated without changing code.
[embed: node/a71b0248-7ca9]
Every screen calls the same forecast endpoint, so the 3D body, charts and alerts always agree.
Tech stack
Frontend: React, Three.js (via React Three Fiber) for the body, Recharts for the curves, Tailwind for styling.
Backend: Python with FastAPI; NumPy and pandas for the model; model values in a JSON settings file.
AI explanations (P1): a language model API that turns each forecast into a short plain-language note.
Hosting: Vercel for the frontend and Render or Railway for the API; free tiers are enough for the demo.
3D model: a free anatomy model in glTF format, with bone and muscle groups as separate meshes so each can be colored.
UX and demo design
The whole demo happens on one screen: a glowing 3D body in the center, with the mission timeline along the bottom. Judges should understand the product within 10 seconds of seeing it.
Screen layout
Center: a 3D astronaut body. Bones and muscles fade from blue (healthy) to orange and red (weakened) and visibly thin as the timeline moves forward.
Bottom: a mission timeline scrubber from launch to 90 days after landing, with markers for the injury, spacewalks and landing.
Left panel: the crew profile and the injury picker.
Right panel: countermeasure sliders, the three risk scores and line charts for bone, muscle and fitness.
Top bar: crew switcher and a "Mission: ISS / Moon / Mars" toggle that adds a communication-delay badge.
The 3-minute demo script
Meet the crew member: day 1, all tissues blue, mission length 180 days.
Press play: the body slowly turns orange as standard exercise only partly holds back the loss.
Day 40, ankle sprain: the left leg turns red and the landing-day risk score turns red.
Ask "what if": drag the sliders to a modified upper-body and single-leg exercise plan plus more protein. The curves split into before and after lines.
Show the result: landing-day risk moves from red to yellow, with a sentence explaining why.
Switch to Mars: a 20-minute delay badge appears and the crew gets the plan on board.
Close with Earth mode: the same body, now a patient in hospital bed rest.
Design style: a dark mission-control theme, one accent color, large clear numbers and smooth animations. Avoid clutter: the body and one before-and-after comparison carry the story.
