Risks and mitigations
The biggest risk is credibility: judges will ask how a weekend model can predict a human body. The answer is to be open about what the model is, show its sources and show its limits.
Risk
Why it matters
Mitigation
Little human data is public
Real astronaut records are private and samples are small
Use published averages and animal and bed rest data; label it a research prototype
Model looks too simple
Judges may doubt a formula-based forecast
Show each assumption and its citation; show an uncertainty band on every curve
Medical claims overreach
A health tool that sounds like a diagnosis loses trust
Clear wording: forecasts and suggestions, approved by a clinician
The 3D body takes too long to build
It is the centerpiece of the demo
Use a free ready-made body model; fall back to a 2D body outline with colored zones
Scope creep
Too many features and nothing polished
Lock the P0 list at hour 4; stretch goals only after hour 36
The live demo fails
A broken demo can sink the pitch
Record a backup video; keep a local copy that works offline
Privacy in a real product
Health data is very sensitive
Plan for encryption, access by role and crew consent in the roadmap

Roadmap and Space Apps fit
Roadmap
Hackathon prototype: forecasts from published averages, an injury module, countermeasure simulator and the 3D body.
Research pilot: calibrate against bed rest study data with a university space medicine lab; add uncertainty estimates.
Commercial stations: a fleet dashboard and an API for station operators and private mission companies, whose crews include older and less-trained flyers.
Moon and Mars: an on-board version that runs without the ground and learns from each crew member's own sensor data.
Earth spin-off: the same model for patients on long bed rest, in intensive care or with limited mobility.
